+# Test suite performance notes
+
+Living document with profiling results, decisions and plans for speeding up
+the RSpec suite. Update it after every optimization phase.
+
+Scope: system specs (`spec/system`) and streaming specs (`:streaming`) are
+excluded from all measurements (they are not run by default).
+
+Phases:
+
+1. `spec/models` — **done** (phase 1)
+2. `spec/requests` — **done** (phase 2)
+3. factories (`spec/fabricators`) — **done** (phase 3)
+
+## How to profile
+
+`test-prof`, `stackprof` and `ruby-prof` are in the Gemfile (`require: false`).
+
+```sh
+# Wall-clock time per example / group
+bundle exec rspec spec/models --profile 30
+
+# Sampling profiler for the whole run (open with `bundle exec stackprof <dump> --limit 40`)
+TEST_STACK_PROF=1 TEST_STACK_PROF_MODE=wall bundle exec rspec spec/models -r test-prof
+bundle exec stackprof tmp/test_prof/stack-prof-report-wall-raw-total.dump --method 'Some#method'
+
+# Time spent in inline Sidekiq jobs
+EVENT_PROF="sidekiq.inline" bundle exec rspec spec/models -r test-prof
+```
+
+NOTE: `EVENT_PROF=factory.create` / `FPROF=1` report nothing when only
+`-r test-prof` is passed (fabrication is not loaded yet at that time). Load
+the Rails helper first, then test-prof — this works for every test-prof tool:
+
+```sh
+# Factory usage (total / top-level count and time per fabricator)
+FPROF=1 bundle exec rspec spec/models -r ./spec/rails_helper -r test-prof
+# Examples creating records they never need in the DB (many false positives for media specs)
+FDOC=1 bundle exec rspec spec/models -r ./spec/rails_helper -r test-prof
+# Setup time (before / let) per example group
+RD_PROF=1 bundle exec rspec spec/requests -r ./spec/rails_helper -r test-prof
+```
+
+Small ad-hoc instrumentation scripts that were useful (load them with
+`-r ./config/environment -r /path/to/script.rb`):
+
+- SQL time aggregated by statement (`ActiveSupport::Notifications.monotonic_subscribe('sql.active_record')`)
+  → SQL itself is only ~12s of the models run, the DB is not the bottleneck.
+- Sidekiq server middleware (`Sidekiq::Testing.server_middleware`) recording
+  job class + duration, and the current example location → finds which jobs
+  run inline and which examples depend on them.
+- An `after` hook dumping `Sidekiq::Worker.jobs` while in fake mode → verifies
+  no example silently stopped running a job it depends on.
+
+The job recorder used in phase 2 (`-r ./config/environment -r ./tmp/prof/job_recorder.rb`):
+
+```ruby
+$job_log = []; $job_current = nil
+class JobRecorderMW
+  def call(_worker, job, _queue)
+    t = Process.clock_gettime(Process::CLOCK_MONOTONIC)
+    yield
+  ensure
+    $job_log << [job['wrapped'] || job['class'], Process.clock_gettime(Process::CLOCK_MONOTONIC) - t, $job_current, Webhook.exists?] if $job_current
+  end
+end
+require 'sidekiq/testing'
+# NOTE: use `c.prepend` to also see jobs skipped by spec/support/sidekiq_webhooks.rb
+Sidekiq::Testing.server_middleware { |c| c.add JobRecorderMW }
+RSpec.configure do |config|
+  # `ex.id` resolves shared examples to the including file
+  config.around { |ex| $job_current = ex.id; ex.run; $job_current = nil }
+  config.after(:suite) { File.write('tmp/prof/jobs.json', JSON.dump($job_log)) }
+end
+```
+
+**Gotcha: never run two rspec processes at the same time** (e.g. one in the
+background and one in the foreground). They share the test Redis DB and the
+global `after { redis.del(redis.keys) }` of one process wipes keys used by
+the other's examples → random failures (rate limits, feeds, `publish`).
+
+## Baseline (before any changes)
+
+Environment: local container, 1 process (no parallelization), Ruby 4.0, Rails 8.1.
+
+| Group         | Examples | Time      |
+|---------------|----------|-----------|
+| `spec/models` | 1795     | **~156s** (2m36s, `Finished in`) |
+
+Top costs in the `spec/models` StackProf (wall) profile:
+
+- ~40% `ActiveRecord::LogSubscriber#sql` — SQL debug logging, mostly
+  `ActiveSupport::BacktraceCleaner` resolving the query call site, because
+  **`ActiveRecord.verbose_query_logs = true`** was set at load time by
+  `spec/models/concerns/status/fetch_replies_concern_spec.rb` (debug leftover
+  that leaked into the whole process, i.e. every spec loaded together with
+  it). The test log grew by >130MB per models run.
+- ~30% inline Sidekiq jobs (`Sidekiq.testing!(:inline)` before every example).
+  19s of 24.6s were `TriggerWebhookWorker` (3.8k jobs): every local
+  account/status/report created by a fabricator triggers the webhook worker,
+  which serializes the event with `REST::Admin::WebhookEventSerializer`, even
+  though no `Webhook` records exist in almost any spec.
+- ~7% ffmpeg/imagemagick subprocesses (media attachment specs; legitimate).
+- ~5% bcrypt (password hashing in the `:user` fabricator; Devise stretches
+  are already 1 in test → bcrypt minimum cost).
+
+## Phase 1: `spec/models` changes
+
+1. **Removed the leaking `ActiveRecord.verbose_query_logs = true`** from
+   `fetch_replies_concern_spec.rb`.
+2. **`spec/support/logging.rb`**: the test logger level defaults to `:warn`
+   (no per-query SQL formatting/writing at all — the AR log subscriber checks
+   `logger.debug?` per event). The level is changed instead of replacing the
+   logger, so specs stubbing `Rails.logger` keep working.
+   - `LOG=1` / `LOG=debug` restores debug logging to `log/test.log`
+   - `VERBOSE_QUERY_LOGS=1` additionally enables query call-site annotations
+   This is process-wide, so it also speeds up all other spec types (no
+   behaviour change for them).
+3. **Sidekiq testing mode via metadata** (`spec/rails_helper.rb`):
+   - `sidekiq: :inline` / `sidekiq: :fake` metadata selects the mode;
+   - without metadata, spec types in `SIDEKIQ_FAKE_BY_DEFAULT_TYPES`
+     (`%i(model)` in phase 1, `%i(model request)` since phase 2) default to `:fake`, every other type still
+     defaults to `:inline` (unchanged behaviour);
+   - explicit `Sidekiq.testing!(:fake)` calls inside specs still work (they
+     run after the global hook);
+   - rspec-sidekiq clears `Sidekiq::Worker.jobs` before each example.
+   Groups/examples whose assertions depend on job side effects were tagged
+   `sidekiq: :inline`. They were found by recording, under the old inline
+   mode, every example that ran any job other than `TriggerWebhookWorker`
+   (or `TriggerWebhookWorker` while `Webhook` records existed — never
+   happened). That list was used instead of "whatever fails in fake mode",
+   because negative assertions (e.g. "does not deliver welcome email")
+   would silently pass in fake mode. Tagged:
+   - whole files: `account_suggestions/fasp_source`, `admin/account_action`,
+     `admin/moderation_action`, `announcement_reaction`, `announcement`,
+     `email_subscription`, `fasp/backfill_request`, `follow_request`,
+     `form/account_batch`, `terms_of_service`, `user/confirmation`
+   - `user_spec`: `#approve!`, `#reset_password!`, `#mark_email_as_confirmed!`,
+     `#active_for_authentication?`
+   - `account_spec`: `searchable`; `tag_feed_spec`: "can restrict to an account"
+   **When adding model specs relying on side effects of jobs (mails via
+   `deliver_later`, notifications, workers called from callbacks), tag them
+   with `sidekiq: :inline`.**
+
+### Result
+
+| Group         | Before | After      | Improvement |
+|---------------|--------|------------|-------------|
+| `spec/models` | ~156s  | **~55s** (54–57s over several seeds) | ~65% faster (2.8x) |
+
+Intermediate: logging fix alone → ~80–94s; + Sidekiq fake mode → ~55s.
+
+## Remaining hot spots in `spec/models` (ideas for later)
+
+- `media_attachment_spec.rb` (~6s): real ffmpeg/imagemagick processing — legit,
+  could share processed attachments with `before_all` if needed.
+- `account_statuses_cleanup_policy_spec.rb` (~4.4s / 36 ex) and
+  `tag_feed_spec.rb` (~3.8s), `trends/statuses_spec.rb` (~3.3s),
+  `public_feed_spec.rb`: big `let!` setups recreated for each example —
+  candidates for `let_it_be`/`before_all` (test-prof), but needs care with
+  time-relative data (`1.year.ago`) and examples mutating records.
+- ~~Factories (phase 3)~~ (done, see phase 3): `Fabricate(:user)` creates an account + keypair (static test RSA keys, no generation) + 2 `login_activity` records + bcrypt
+  hashing; `:account`/`:status` cascades. Look at `FactoryProf`-like stats
+  (`EVENT_PROF=factory.create` with test-prof loaded from rails_helper).
+- GC ~7% of samples.
+
+## Phase 2: `spec/requests`
+
+### Baseline
+
+| Group           | Examples | Original behaviour (`LOG=debug`) | After phase 1 (log level `:warn`) |
+|-----------------|----------|----------------------------------|-----------------------------------|
+| `spec/requests` | 1877     | ~176s                            | ~160–164s                         |
+
+(`LOG=debug` approximates the original logging behaviour; the
+verbose-query-logs leak only applied when the models spec file was loaded.)
+
+Overall goal: `spec/models` + `spec/requests` at least 2x faster than the
+original baseline: (156s + 176s) = ~332s → **≤ ~166s**.
+
+### Profile after phase 1 (StackProf wall, `spec/requests`, ~162s)
+
+- **~30% `Sidekiq::Client#push`** (request specs still ran all jobs inline):
+  - 21.6% `TriggerWebhookWorker` (6.7k jobs, ~35s; `Webhook.exists?` was
+    false in every single one of them);
+  - mail delivery jobs (~7s, mostly Premailer CSS inlining),
+    `LocalNotificationWorker` (~7s), `DistributionWorker` (~5s).
+- 61% inside `Fabricate` (incl. the jobs above triggered from callbacks) —
+  per-example `let`/`before` setup; factories are phase 3.
+- Only ~15–18% is actual request processing (`Integration::Session#process`).
+- ~3% `Rails.application.reload_routes!` (+ `define_url_helper`) — routes
+  redrawn & fully reloaded around each of 30 examples in
+  `signature_verification_spec.rb`.
+- `cache_spec.rb`: 258 examples (~34s) each recreating the same records.
+- ~3% bcrypt (`:user` fabricator), ~7% GC.
+
+### Changes
+
+1. **Sidekiq fake mode by default for request specs**:
+   `SIDEKIQ_FAKE_BY_DEFAULT_TYPES = %i(model request)` (`spec/rails_helper.rb`).
+   Dependent files were found with the job recorder (above): every example
+   that ran any job other than `TriggerWebhookWorker`-without-webhooks under
+   inline mode. 188 examples in 46 files; whole files were tagged
+   `sidekiq: :inline` on the top-level `describe` (narrower tagging would only
+   save ~4s more; the notification/timeline specs, which are the most
+   expensive ones, depend on jobs in nearly every example anyway):
+   `activitypub/{featured_collections,inboxes}`, `api/fasp/data_sharing/v0/continuations`,
+   `api/v1/accounts/{credentials,email_subscriptions,statuses}`, `api/v1/accounts`,
+   `api/v1/admin/{account_actions,accounts,domain_blocks}`, `api/v1/announcements/reactions`,
+   `api/v1/{collection_items,collections,conversations,directories,domain_blocks}`,
+   `api/v1/emails/confirmations`, `api/v1/{featured_tags,follow_requests}`,
+   `api/v1/lists/accounts`, `api/v1/markers`, `api/v1/notifications/requests`,
+   `api/v1/notifications`, `api/v1/polls/votes`, `api/v1/reports`,
+   `api/v1/statuses/{favourites,pins,quotes,reblogs}`, `api/v1/statuses`,
+   `api/v1/tags`, `api/v1/timelines/{home,list,tag}`, `api/v2/media`,
+   `api/v2/notifications/accounts`, `api/v2/notifications`, `api/v2/{search,suggestions}`,
+   `auth/confirmations`, `collection_items`, `collections`,
+   `email_subscriptions/confirmations`, `settings/pictures`,
+   `signature_verification`, `statuses`.
+   Verified afterwards with the fake-mode `Sidekiq::Worker.jobs` dump: the only
+   untagged examples that enqueue non-webhook jobs are those calling
+   `Sidekiq.testing!(:fake)` themselves and asserting on the enqueued job.
+   **When adding request specs relying on side effects of jobs, tag them
+   `sidekiq: :inline`.**
+2. **Skip pointless `TriggerWebhookWorker` runs in inline mode**
+   (`spec/support/sidekiq_webhooks.rb`): a `Sidekiq::Testing.server_middleware`
+   skips the job unless an enabled `Webhook` subscribes to the event (same
+   check as `WebhookService#webhooks_for_event`, i.e. no observable difference;
+   webhook specs create `Webhook` records first). Applies to all spec types
+   running jobs inline (incl. the `sidekiq: :inline` groups, ~9s in requests).
+   `SIDEKIQ_RUN_WEBHOOK_TRIGGERS=1` disables it. Fake mode is not affected
+   (server middleware only runs when jobs are executed), so
+   `have_enqueued_sidekiq_job(TriggerWebhookWorker)` assertions still work.
+3. **`before_all` / `let_it_be` (test-prof) enabled** in `spec/rails_helper.rb`,
+   plus a `TestProf::BeforeAll` `:begin` hook that applies the group's
+   Sidekiq testing mode (`sidekiq_testing_mode_for(metadata)`), since
+   `before_all` runs before per-example hooks.
+   - `cache_spec.rb`: the top-level read-only setup (alice, moderator user,
+     statuses, poll, invite, token, follow) is now `before_all` (once for 258
+     examples): ~22s → ~6s. The setup writes one Redis key
+     (`activity:statuses:local:<week>`, read only by `/api/v1/instance/activity`
+     which the file does not request); Redis/`Rails.cache` are still cleared
+     after each example.
+   - **Caveat**: Redis/`Rails.cache` are wiped after every example, so only
+     move setup into `before_all` if it doesn't rely on Redis/cache state
+     (check with `redis.keys` right after the setup).
+4. **`signature_verification_spec.rb`**: test routes are drawn once in
+   `before(:all)` and app routes reloaded once in `after(:all)` (instead of
+   draw + full `reload_routes!` around each example); the controller is
+   still `stub_const`-ed per example (routes reference it by name).
+   `reload_routes_unless_loaded` is called before drawing, otherwise the lazy
+   route set would load the real routes on the first request and drop the
+   test routes.
+
+### Result
+
+| Group           | Original | After phase 1 | After phase 2 | vs. original |
+|-----------------|----------|---------------|---------------|--------------|
+| `spec/requests` | ~176s    | ~160–164s     | **~92–98s** (several seeds) | ~45% faster |
+| `spec/models`   | ~156s    | ~55s          | ~56s (re-checked) | ~64% faster |
+| **sum**         | ~332s    | ~215s         | **~150s**     | **~2.2x** |
+
+Intermediate (requests): fake Sidekiq mode → ~136s; + `before_all` in
+`cache_spec` + routes fix → ~110s; + webhook trigger skipping → ~92–98s.
+
+Also re-checked (one folder at a time): `spec/controllers`, `spec/services`,
+`spec/workers`, `spec/lib` pass.
+
+## Remaining hot spots in `spec/requests` (ideas for later)
+
+- Factories dominate (~60% of samples are inside `Fabricate`): the
+  `with API authentication` shared context creates a user (+ account,
+  2 `login_activity` rows, bcrypt) and a token in every example. Candidate for
+  phase 3 (cheaper `:user` fabricator) or `let_it_be` where examples don't
+  mutate the user.
+- `activitypub/replies_spec.rb` (~10s / 45 ex): 6 statuses (+10 in some
+  contexts) per example, parametrized by `let(:parent_visibility)` → would
+  need restructuring into `let_it_be` per context.
+- `api/v{1,2}/notifications_spec.rb` (~15s together): run inline jobs
+  (notifications are created by `LocalNotificationWorker`, plus mails with
+  Premailer) — could build notifications directly, but that changes the
+  setup semantics; left as is.
+- `activitypub/contexts_spec.rb` "with many statuses" (~1.6s single example),
+  `api/v1/admin/measures_spec.rb`.
+
+## Phase 3: factories (`spec/fabricators`)
+
+### Baseline (FactoryProf, `FPROF=1 ... -r ./spec/rails_helper -r test-prof`)
+
+| Group           | Time   | Factory time | Fabricate calls (total / top-level) |
+|-----------------|--------|--------------|-------------------------------------|
+| `spec/models`   | ~55.6s | 45.7s        | 4721 / 3243                         |
+| `spec/requests` | ~101s  | 53.9s        | 8203 / 4762                         |
+
+(Requests measured ~101s this time vs ~92–98s at the end of phase 2, which is
+normal machine variance; compare numbers measured in the same session.)
+
+Findings:
+
+- **Hidden cascade in `:status`**: `after_create { Fabricate(:bookmark, status: status) }`.
+  Every created status also created a bookmark, **plus a new local account
+  (with user, keypair, bcrypt) for the bookmark**: ~1000 extra bookmarks per
+  group, ~10.5s in each of models and requests; `Fabricate(:status)` took
+  40 SQL queries instead of ~20. No spec depended on it (checked every spec
+  mentioning bookmarks; bookmark specs create their own bookmarks).
+- **Hidden cascade in `:user`**: `after_create { 2.times { Fabricate(:login_activity, user: user) } }`
+  → 2456 extra `login_activity` rows in requests (~2s). No spec depended on
+  it (login activity specs create their own records).
+  Both callbacks are not in upstream Mastodon's fabricators.
+- bcrypt for the default fabricator password: ~1ms (~10%) of every local
+  account/user creation.
+- What's left is legitimate: `account` ~8–10ms (15 queries: uniqueness
+  validations, keypair, user), `media_attachment` ~150ms (real
+  ffmpeg/imagemagick processing, almost all in `media_attachment_spec`).
+- FactoryDoctor (models): only ~6.6s of potential waste and ~4.3s of that is
+  `media_attachment_spec` false positives (processing, not SQL); the rest
+  is spread across ~100 examples with a few ms each → not worth per-example
+  refactoring.
+
+### Changes
+
+1. `spec/fabricators/status_fabricator.rb`: removed the `after_create`
+   bookmark cascade. **Create bookmarks explicitly (`Fabricate(:bookmark, ...)`) where needed.**
+2. `spec/fabricators/user_fabricator.rb`: removed the `after_create`
+   login-activity cascade. **Create `login_activity` explicitly where needed.**
+3. `spec/support/password_digest_cache.rb`: `User#password_digest` is prepended
+   to reuse a single bcrypt digest for the fabricator's default password
+   (`'123456789'`, keep in sync with the fabricator). It is still a real bcrypt
+   hash of that password; any other password is hashed normally.
+
+**Rule for fabricators: no `after_create` creating unrelated records.**
+Default associations should be built only when required (prefer
+`Fabricate.build` for `belongs_to`), and optional data should be opt-in via
+explicit attributes/transients or separate fabricators (`from:`).
+
+### Result
+
+| Group           | Phase 3 baseline | After phase 3 | Improvement |
+|-----------------|------------------|---------------|-------------|
+| `spec/models`   | ~55.6s           | **~41–43s** (41.3, 41.5, 42.0) | ~25% |
+| `spec/requests` | ~101s            | **~81s** (81.2, 81.8)          | ~20% |
+| **sum**         | ~157s            | **~123s**                      | ~22% |
+
+Original (pre-phase-1) sum was ~332s → now ~123s (**~2.7x faster**).
+
+Intermediate: cascade removal alone → models 43.4s, requests 86.4s; + digest
+cache → 41.5s / 81.2s.
+
+Factory stats after: models 3449 / 3243 calls, 34.3s; requests 4811 / 4762
+calls, 40.6s (total ≈ top-level now, i.e. no hidden cascades left).
+
+Also re-checked (one folder at a time, all green): `spec/controllers`,
+`spec/services`, `spec/workers`, `spec/lib`, `spec/presenters`,
+`spec/serializers`, `spec/policies`, `spec/validators`, `spec/helpers`,
+`spec/mailers`, `spec/fabrication`, `spec/views`, `spec/config`,
+`spec/routing`, `spec/chewy`, `spec/locales`.
+
+### Ideas for later (not factory-level, bigger refactorings)
+
+- RSpecDissect (requests): setup is ~57s of ~81s, spread across many files;
+  biggest: `api/v{1,2}/notifications_spec.rb` (~10.6s `before`, inline jobs
+  creating notifications), `activitypub/replies_spec.rb` (~3.8s),
+  `cache_spec.rb` (~3.7s, but that's the request itself in `before`).
+- `with API authentication` (`let(:user)` + token per example, ~930 users in
+  requests ≈ 10s): `let_it_be` would help, but many examples mutate the user
+  (settings, roles, `account` state) → needs per-file opt-in (e.g. a
+  `user_fabricator:`-like shared context option) rather than a global change.
+- Account creation itself (~8ms): uniqueness validations + keypair + user
+  insert; reducing it would require app-level changes (out of scope).
